WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.320
Film Courage: Gentlemen, for your new movie
DON’T K*** IT which is out now, did your

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:08.870
distributor have a demand for how many zombies
were in the film?

00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:10.499
Was there a zombie count that you had to meet?

00:00:10.499 --> 00:00:11.499
Mike Mendez: No.

00:00:11.499 --> 00:00:14.480
I would have to phrase it more that they indulged
me on that.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.090
They were kind enough to not get in the way.

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:23.710
That was a prerequisite for wanting to do
it was how many people can we get in a 85-minute

00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:24.710
running time.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:26.970
They were very kind to let me do it.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.410
Dolph Lundgren: Yeah.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.929
I think it was…they wanted no less than
300 wasn’t it?

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:33.480
Mike Mendez: I think so.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.480
Yeah.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:35.480
Totally.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.629
It’s one of my favorite things when someone
lets me make a movie because I always feel

00:00:39.629 --> 00:00:45.870
like I’ll say something like “So then
you toy with the zombie and you lift them

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:50.810
and I’m sort of waiting for someone to stop
me because in my mind I’m like “I can’t

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.570
believe they’re letting me do this!”

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:53.570
[Laughs].

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.980
And that is always a good feeling that we’re
on to something good.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.789
Film Courage: Any other requests that the
distributor asked?

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:04.360
Certain scenes…I know…without a shirt
on…were there certain things that they [requested]…I

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.880
mean in all seriousness, that they wanted
as part of the film?

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:07.880
Dolph Lundgren: No.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:08.880
They were very nice actually.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.420
They were very easy to work with creatively.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:14.189
I think one thing about this project is everybody
loved the script.

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:18.729
And Mike did a little polish on it to make
it better.

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:21.770
Just added on the bar scene in the beginning
to make it better.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:26.619
The distributors were quite good about everything
except it had to be shot in 17 days.

00:01:26.619 --> 00:01:29.320
That was the only problem.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:35.520
So I think you know it was more financial
issues but on the creative issue, there was

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:36.520
really nothing.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:37.520
Mike Mendez: They were super supportive.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.130
Dolph Lundgren: Yeah, they were very cool.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.569
Mike Mendez: The challenge was budgetary.

00:01:40.569 --> 00:01:41.569
Time is always the enemy.

00:01:41.569 --> 00:01:42.869
But they were very much on board.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:48.260
And I was surprised because sometimes you
get producers who want to impose their will.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.020
But they let us make the movie that we wanted
to make which is very rare and I really thank

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.020
them for that.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.950
Film Courage: I’m wondering who hired whom.

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:58.150
[Dolph] I know you’re an actor/producer
[on this project] and Mike I know you’re

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:59.150
the director.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:00.860
And [Dolph] you were in the crowdfunding [Indiegogo
campaign] video.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.840
So who hired whom? Dolph Lundgren: I think
I hired myself first?

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.549
I read the script.

00:02:06.549 --> 00:02:08.880
I thought it was good.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.030
Got involved with the producers.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:12.030
Met with Mike.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.050
It all happened concurrently.

00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:17.600
As usual with these independent movies, it’s
not a simple shot.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.500
There was a producer who was side railed.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.780
He wasn’t part of it anymore.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:23.780
Mike Mendez: Right.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.420
He passed away, did you know that?

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.490
Dolph Lundgren: Yeah, I heard that.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:31.210
But it was like I met Mike and I read the
script and I really thought it was great.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:37.290
I then I tried to support the filmmakers in
their endeavor including raising funds for

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.290
it.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.850
Film Courage: It’s clear that there is passion
behind it and that you [both] wanted to be

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.880
involved in it, you were in the Indiegogo
video, etc.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:50.540
But I have to take another sort of turn and
that is to say that in this industry you may

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:54.940
take a job that you don’t want to do, how
is that for either of you?

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.920
And please don’t name project that you that
you didn’t want to be a part of.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.300
But ones [projects] that you’ve been a part
of because you feel like you have to keep

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.300
the [career] engine going.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.570
Dolph Lundgren: You mean like THE EXPENDABLES?

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:04.610
Film Courage: No, no, no.

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:05.610
I just meant…

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.610
Dolph Lundgren: I’m just kidding!

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:07.610
[Laughs] Film Courage: No.

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:08.610
I wasn’t going there.

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:12.200
But everyone has to do certain jobs they don’t
want to do and know that it is part of ‘The

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.200
Machine.’

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.940
And how is it for the two of you knowing that
it is part of being in this business and staying

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:18.940
relevant?

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.570
Dolph Lundgren: Well…I can start then…you
know, look.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.510
It’s all in your inner attitude.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:31.090
You say you don’t want to do it, well that’s
being kind of simplistic about life because

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:35.460
you can always find something good in any
experience, even a bad experience.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.250
Something you think is bad could be a good
experience.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:40.110
You don’t even know that until years later,
perhaps.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.910
Or weeks later or days later.

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:47.030
Sometimes the bad things in life where you
are being tried are the things that make you

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:48.030
grow.

00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:49.340
I mean I look at it that way.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:55.610
Say if I need to pay off my divorce, for instance
and I need to do a couple of movies, I always

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.700
try to find something good in each of those
roles.

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:02.160
Even if perhaps I was sitting on a pile of
money and didn’t have to take that job,

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.060
then maybe I would have waited around for
something better.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:10.530
But what you think is better, a lot of actors
could sit around and wait for a long time

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:11.820
and nothing happens.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:17.100
So sometimes you can do something and out
of that energy comes something that you can’t

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.100
even imagine, you know?

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.120
That’s just how life is.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.440
So I just take any role where I try to make
the best of it and try to enjoy it, that’s

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.440
how I look at it.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.570
And this movie wasn’t one of those pictures.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:33.810
This was something that I wanted to do but
I do stuff sometimes that I’m not 100% sure

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:34.810
about.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.260
But most of the time afterwards I’m glad
I did it.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:39.970
I always think that.

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:41.730
Because something good always happens.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.950
Film Courage: So even if you’re dreading
when the alarm goes off you can always find

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:47.700
some kind of gift that maybe you can learn?

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.080
Dolph Lundgren: I’m not dreading it.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:51.090
But you may be less excited about it.

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:56.510
Maybe you meet somebody on that set or you
learn something as an actor or there is some

00:04:56.510 --> 00:05:00.630
revelation you come to by going through the
hardship…it’s part of life.

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:04.550
It’s part of the experience of this life.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:08.740
Mike Mendez: And I guess fortunately for me
or unfortunately, I don’t find myself in

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:09.920
that position too often.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.940
Because it’s not like people offer me large
sums of money to do anything [laughs] so I

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:18.930
have to kind of find a project, you know?

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:22.650
Because these are low-budget films and you
kind of want to find the best thing that suits

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:23.650
you.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:28.760
The best is when something is not particularly
a great script, they are not offering you

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:34.370
a lot of money and there are very few perks,
it’s quite easy to say “No, thank you.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.400
Perhaps another time.”

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.360
So you’re kind of just trying to find the
best project that excites you, that you can

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.780
find something good about it and try to put
your best foot forward and try to make the

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:49.620
most out of the situation.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:56.110
So kind of like Dolph is saying, trying to
find the silver lining and if there is a thing,

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:59.680
seriously consider that and try to make the
best thing out of it.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:04.590
Film Courage: Mike according to your IMDB,
you have 24 credits as an editor, 10 as a

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.390
director and only 6 as a writer.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:10.430
It’s surprising to see that you haven't
spent more time writing scripts.

00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:13.280
I’m just curious what the reason is?

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.880
Mike Mendez: It’s a long-winded answer.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:17.150
So why don’t I write scripts?

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:24.060
So when I had my first film in Sundance I
had a lot of writing opportunities.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.760
They all got rejected, you know.

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:34.530
I sort of almost have this reaction that directing
is always rewarded and good.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:36.210
Writing I always feel rejected.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:42.020
And I almost always feel like it’s too much
on you if you write and direct.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:46.330
And so at least…and I don’t know (maybe
this will change one day), I like collaborating

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:48.080
with people.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:52.990
My role as a director (I feel) kind of also
goes with working with a screenwriter.

00:06:52.990 --> 00:06:57.580
The same way I’d work with an actor or a
cinematographer or something like that, it’s

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:58.810
about collaboration.

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:03.650
So I think that part of the key to being a
director is knowing that people can do the

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.150
job better than you.

00:07:05.150 --> 00:07:09.020
And so I feel that there are a lot of people
that are better writers than me, so why not

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:10.340
collaborate with them?

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:17.150
And I still put my stamp on it and what I
like to do by putting my polish and working

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:21.180
with them without having all the pressure
of like “It’s all on me if people hate

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:23.060
the script or the movie.

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:25.650
It’s all on me!”

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:29.630
So I just found that it works better for me,
working with writers.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:35.030
Film Courage: Dolph we have a lot of actors
that watch our [Film Courage Youtube] channel

00:07:35.030 --> 00:07:39.120
and I know you have a fascinating story of
how you got into acting.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:45.080
But in terms of today 2017, what would you
recommend to someone who is back home in Sweden

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:48.449
or somewhere in the United States that wants
to come to Los Angeles to be an actor?

00:07:48.449 --> 00:07:50.270
What would you say to them?

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:55.340
Dolph Lundgren: Well I would say follow your
dreams, follow your instincts.

00:07:55.340 --> 00:08:03.010
What is it Stella Adler said “In order to
be in this business you need the soul of a

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:06.800
rose and the hyde of a rhinoceros.”

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:09.860
So that kind of sums it all up.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:15.770
You are trying to protect that creative part
of you by dealing with a lot of B.S.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.070
every day.

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:22.800
And sometimes you get to express that which
is the reason you get into this business in

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:23.800
the first place.

00:08:23.800 --> 00:08:30.020
So I think people should follow their dreams
and not be afraid of failing and trying again.

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.969
I mean great actors…look at Morgan Freeman
for instance.

00:08:32.969 --> 00:08:35.940
I ran into him not long ago.

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:37.070
Great guy.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:40.050
Nobody knew who he was until he was about
50-55 years old.

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:45.029
He’d done all these movies and suddenly
he was in DRIVING MISS DAISY.

00:08:45.029 --> 00:08:49.580
So there’s a guy who probably had to deal
with a lot of rejection and failure and now

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.380
he’s one of the greatest actors in the country
and the world.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:53.120
So don’t give up.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.310
That’s the thing - don’t give up!

00:08:55.310 --> 00:09:00.180
Film Courage: So if you were going to give
them a pep talk before they came here…because

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.510
I think a lot of people come out here with
the idea, especially if they’re told that

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:06.110
they are great looking back home or whatever.

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:09.270
And then you come here [to Los Angeles] and
there are so many great looking people that

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:14.100
it’s like…it’s almost overwhelming because
you felt special, maybe?

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:15.100
Dolph Lundgren: Yeah.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:16.100
It’s true.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:17.100
It’s true.

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.950
I mean, Los Angeles is the place that everybody
comes to from around the whole world.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.240
And I guess you just have to believe in yourself
and work on your craft.

00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:31.500
It basically comes down to your ability to
entertain people on screen, if you’re in

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.200
the film business or TV business.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.089
When you get your shot, you’ve got to be
ready to take it and then you’ve got to

00:09:38.089 --> 00:09:40.390
really go for it at that point.

00:09:40.390 --> 00:09:44.610
Like I said, there is all that time in between
that is tough.

00:09:44.610 --> 00:09:51.690
What is it, Michael Caine who said “They
pay me to wait.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:52.710
Acting I do for free.”

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:54.280
So there you go.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.180
There is a lot of waiting around.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:02.440
So you just have to stay at it and work out
and stay healthy and focus on your craft and

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:04.900
go for it when you get your shot.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:08.310
Film Courage: I find that interesting in terms
of being ready when something is presented

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:11.460
(and I’ve heard this before to) that most
people aren’t ready.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:12.830
They think they are.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:17.440
Were there ever times for the two of you (maybe
we’ll go back to you Mike first) where you

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:19.460
weren’t sure “Am I actually ready?”

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:23.510
when something came your way and you had to
check yourself and say “This is happening

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:29.500
and ….” Mike Mendez: Well I think that
could be said for every project or every film,

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.110
you know?

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:35.051
Everything you hope is a new challenge to
yourself and you’re trying something that

00:10:35.051 --> 00:10:41.010
is a little different that it’s never…at
least for me I can never just walk through

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:45.490
a movie or just phone it in because there
is always a new challenge and there is always

00:10:45.490 --> 00:10:46.490
a new thing.

00:10:46.490 --> 00:10:49.399
I’ve never worked with a legendary action
hero [gesturing to Dolph].

00:10:49.399 --> 00:10:52.880
So that was like “Okay, how is this going
to go?”

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.290
I had never worked with a giant CGI spider
before!

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:02.610
And so you’re constantly trying to find
new challenges for yourself and you always

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:04.340
question it.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.930
I feel it’s healthy.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:12.950
Most of my friends are filmmakers and I see
it in them.

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:16.589
A lot of them get a big opportunity and are
nervous, even though they might have had a

00:11:16.589 --> 00:11:19.590
lot of success before, they’re nervous.

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:22.350
And what we always kind of say is “That’s
good!”

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:26.720
If you’re not nervous I don’t think you’re
in the right place.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:27.720
You should be nervous.

00:11:27.720 --> 00:11:33.570
You should be questioning if you can do it
and if it’s the right thing because I think

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:37.660
you only progress if you put yourself in that
uncomfortable space.

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:41.340
Sort of like “No, I kind of just like doing
this and I’m just going to do this” I

00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:44.880
think you become complacent and I don’t
think you do interesting and exciting work.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:49.250
So I think it is always important to be kind
of out of your comfort zone.

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:56.810
Dolph Lundgren: And also I want to add that
it’s life that matters, not only what you

00:11:56.810 --> 00:12:00.260
do on film or on television, because it’s
your personal growth.

00:12:00.260 --> 00:12:03.490
Ultimately that is why we are all here.

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:07.320
I mean you can do great work and then you
get fat and take your own life.

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:12.420
After a few years, take your own life or become
a drug addict.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:17.320
And you can still do great work but it was
not such a great life, maybe?

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:19.680
For you or for your children or for your family
or your friends.

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:25.459
So you have to kind of stay close to yourself
and your personal growth while you’re trying

00:12:25.459 --> 00:12:28.340
to get this other stuff done in the business.

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:31.710
And that balance is quite hard to hit.

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:34.070
You know I work on that every day.

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:39.990
And I think that is one of my strengths (perhaps)
is that I’ve stayed close to who I am and

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:43.040
who I was before I got in here in this crazy
town, you know?

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.670
I think that is what people have to remember.

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:52.550
Because that is the big thing that will derail
you: failure or success can screw you up,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:53.550
you know?

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:55.170
It’s the middle way, like Buddha said.

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:56.170
The middle way.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:12:58.110
That’s where you should be most of the time.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.550
That is the healthiest way of existing I think.

00:13:01.550 --> 00:13:07.210
The big stuff is great, but it always goes
away and it can lead to a lot of negatives.

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:08.210
And the really lows, too are tough, as well.

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:11.190
So you try to stay in the middle ground.

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:16.810
Film Courage: How was that for you to do your
(2015) Ted Talk because you showed yourself

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:22.200
as being human and for most people around
the world they see you as superhuman?

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:23.270
How was this for you?

00:13:23.270 --> 00:13:26.940
Because when people do see you they call out
your name as your [various] character(s) “Oh,

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:27.940
Gunner!”

00:13:27.940 --> 00:13:32.690
But you’re actually a human being and you
revealed a lot of human things.

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:33.770
How was that?

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:38.910
Dolph Lundgren: The Ted Talk I did was mostly
about my Dad and my upbringing and the abuse

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:42.130
and things like that and also about trying
to help other people.

00:13:42.130 --> 00:13:45.270
If you help yourself, the next step is for
you help other people.

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:46.900
Yeah, it was cool.

00:13:46.900 --> 00:13:51.360
It was very emotional and it was difficult
to prepare and it was difficult to get up

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:55.950
on stage and stand still and reveal those
things.

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:57.670
But it felt really good, you know.

00:13:57.670 --> 00:13:59.959
And afterwards you really feel like a personal
accomplishment.

00:13:59.959 --> 00:14:06.800
For me, it was one of the most important moments
in my career.

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:09.720
I mean movies are great and that’s what
the fans see.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:11.649
But for me as a person, that was a great moment.

00:14:11.649 --> 00:14:14.060
I’m glad you liked it.

